notes.body:
One issue is that `dissect_mysql_pdu` might throw an exception, in which case the compressed state is never set back.

Here's an idea:

Get rid of the `MYSQL_COMPRESS_PAYLOAD` state entirely. That way, we know that the compression state doesn't change back, it only moves forward, and we can just check `frame_start_compressed`.
Do a check like this in `dissect_mysql()`:

```c
        conversation = find_conversation_pinfo(pinfo, 0);
        if (conversation) {
                conn_data = (mysql_conn_data_t *)conversation_get_proto_data(conversation, proto_mysql);
        }
        if (conn_data && conn_data->compressed_state == MYSQL_COMPRESS_ACTIVE &&
                pinfo->num > conn_data->frame_start_compressed) {
                tcp_dissect_pdus(tvb, pinfo, tree, mysql_desegment, 3,
                         get_mysql_pdu_len, mysql_dissect_compressed, data);
        } else {
                tcp_dissect_pdus(tvb, pinfo, tree, mysql_desegment, 3,
                         get_mysql_pdu_len, dissect_mysql_pdu, data);
        }
```

(Possibly even just pass conn_data as data to `tcp_dissect_pdus` so that `get_mysql_pdu_len` can use it.)

Change `mysql_dissect_compressed` to have the signature of a normal dissector, so it can be passed into `tcp_dissect_pdus`.

Now `mysql_dissect_compressed` is always called directly for a compressed header, and when `dissect_mysql_pdu` is called we know it should be treated as uncompressed. So get rid of the test in `dissect_mysql_pdu` for `conn_data->compressed_state == MYSQL_COMPRESS_ACTIVE` that calls `mysql_dissect_compressed` - it wouldn't be needed anymore. Similarly, remove the calls here in `mysql_dissect_compressed` that change the compressed state, so we don't have to worry about setting them back.

Probably add this to `mysql_dissect_compressed`:
```c
        ti = proto_tree_add_item(tree, proto_mysql, tvb, offset, -1, ENC_NA);
        mysql_tree = proto_item_add_subtree(ti, ett_mysql);
```

This could then eventually lead to registering another dissector that always calls `tcp_dissect_pdus()` with `mysql_dissect_compressed()` directly (setting up the connection data if it doesn't exit), handy for captures that have compressed MySQL but start after the handshake that sets it up. (Might need two dissectors, one for zlib and one for zstd, or I suppose a preference.)

Does that make sense?

